  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Tuesday)\nIssued at 142 PM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a strong upper wave lifting NE, crossing \nthe Dakotas/eastern MT. Surface analysis shows a surface low across \nnorth-central WY. Low level SE flow is drawing increased moisture \nacross central SD, wrapping backwards towards the plains east and \nnorth of the Black Hills. Dewpoints are varying widely...from the \n40s across parts of NE WY to the 50s over far western SD to around \n70 over far SCentral SD. A dryline boundary south of the surface low \nis expected to slide eastward across SW SD/Neb panhandle late this \nafternoon and evening. This low level forcing will combine with an \nupper wave, supporting thunderstorm development. The best \ninstability will be ahead of the dryline across SCentral SD, with \nMLCAPE reaching 2500 J/kg. Shear is decent enough for supercells, \nwith large hail and damaging winds. Farther west across the SD \nplains where higher Theta E air has drawn in: some isolated \nthunderstorms are possible, but not enough instability for severe \nweather. Severe storms will exit the eastern border of the CWA by \nmid-evening but could see lingering showers over NE WY/NW SD \nlingering for a few more hours. Then low amplitude ridging sets up \nacross the region overnight into Wednesday morning, resulting in \nstrong warm air advection. Sunny skies will return and temperatures \nare expected to top out in the mid 90s across portions of the \nwestern SD plains. Temperatures well above average will continue \nthrough the end of the week...which could be in the upper 90s \nFri/Sat. While isolated thunderstorms can't be ruled out most days, \nthe pattern will be generally dry for the second half of the week.",
